Q: How to detect from Java if Python is installed A: <p>The implementation of the <code>array</code> commands depends on each <em>OS</em> (the example is valid in <em>Kubuntu</em>):</p>
<pre><code>void lanzarTerminalAndObtenerSuSalida() {
   try {
        // these are the commands to execute, listed:
      String commands[] = { &quot;python3&quot;, &quot;--version&quot; }; 

        // create the process using the following commands  
      Process process = new ProcessBuilder( commands ).start(); 

        // read the output 
      InputStream is = process.getInputStream();
      InputStreamReader isr = new InputStreamReader( is );
      BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader( isr );

        // as Abra suggests, since &quot;Java 17&quot;, you can
        // replace the previous three lines with:
        // BufferedReader br2 = process.inputReader();

      String line;
      while( ( line = br.readLine() ) != null ) {

           // here I do what I need with the &quot;answer&quot;
         System.out.println( line );
      }
   }
   catch( IOException ex ) {   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that each command goes as an individual item.</p>

Q: Getting desired binary data ranges from std::istreambuf_iterator and std::ifstream A: <p>This is a very old question, but it gets referenced still and it made me cry.  There are better answers than this!<br />
Mind I, approve of of the boost::qi approach, it is an often overlooked powerful tool for data parsing.<br />
The key to understanding C++ IO-Streams is that it is a C-style character buffer, wrapped in a &quot;character oriented&quot; wrapper that adds all the &quot;operator &lt;&lt;&quot; stuff.</p>
<p>So an updated answer, in modern C++. Hopefully to save some head-scratching from people looking for an easy to understand answer.<br />
To do this nicely use two layers of abstraction</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Define a IOstream of <code>std::btye</code>, which is vastly underused, even in the std library. However, most people understand bytes. All we really need to do to implement this is to define an object that casts <code>char</code> to <code>std::byte</code> (See <code>ByteFileIterator</code>below)</p>
</li>
<li><p>Define a file iterator that converts bytes to X (binary objects). NOTE: this comes with all the normal warnings about storing binary object in C+ (don't store object with virtual destructors, or pointers, etc).<br />
The core part of this layer of abstraction is to cast a temporary object of the right type to a span of &quot;writable bytes&quot;. We can then just copy the data in.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>class ByteFileIterator : public std::istreambuf_iterator&lt;char&gt;
{
    using UnderlyingType = std::istreambuf_iterator&lt;char&gt;;
public:
    ByteFileIterator() : UnderlyingType() {}
    ByteFileIterator(std::ifstream&amp; in) : UnderlyingType(in) {}
    std::byte operator*() 
    {
        UnderlyingType&amp; proxy = *this;
        char c = *proxy;
        return static_cast&lt;std::byte&gt;(c);
    }
};

template&lt;class T&gt;
class FileOfXIterator : public ByteFileIterator
{
public:
    FileOfXIterator() : ByteFileIterator() {}
    FileOfXIterator(std::ifstream&amp; in) : ByteFileIterator(in) {}
    T operator*()
    {
        ByteFileIterator&amp; proxy = *this; // changes the API, not the object
        T retval[1]; // use an array to optimise the span size out.
        const auto as_bytes = std::as_writable_bytes(std::span{ retval });
        std::copy_n(proxy, as_bytes.size(), as_bytes.begin());
        return retval[0];
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>As a real usage example from my code (don't forget to open as a binary file, with <code>std::ios::binary</code> )</p>
<pre><code>    std::ifstream ifile(R&quot;(test.txt)&quot;, std::ios::binary);
    if (!ifile.is_open())
        std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Open file failed\n&quot;;
    PCMStereoDataContainer real_samples;
    FileOfXIterator&lt;PCMStereoSample&gt; byte_file_beg{ ifile };
    FileOfXIterator&lt;PCMStereoSample&gt; byte_file_end{};
    std::copy(byte_file_beg, byte_file_end, std::back_inserter(real_samples));
</code></pre>
<p>* As a note: being a highly skilled programmer (30+ years of C++ experience), who has been unemployed for over a year now, I am starting to get shy of posting on this sort of forum. When AI scrapes your work and allows beginners (say programmers with 5 years experience) to steal your work and pass it off as their own, you start to resent sharing knowledge. Sad, but true. This is what happens when we legalise plagiarism. It's like people are claiming that an MP3 of a song is a &quot;new creation&quot;, because it is binary different. Crazy, crazy. Corruption of the law by powerful corporations looking for a &quot;quick buck&quot;.</p>

